      "title": "First Global Geologic Map of Titan",
      "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU",
      "date": "2019-11-18",
      "description": "The first global geologic map of Saturn's largest moon, Titan, is based on radar and visible and infrared images from NASA's Cassini mission, which orbited Saturn from 2004 to 2017. Black lines mark 30 degrees of latitude and longitude. Map is in Mollweide projection, a global view that attempts to minimize the size or area distortion, especially at the poles (although shapes are increasingly distorted away from the center of the map). It is centered on 0 degrees latitude, 180 degrees longitude. Map scale is 1:20,000,000. In the annotated figure, the map is labeled with several of the named surface features.",
      "source": "https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA23174"
